Appearance: Mercurius possesses an elegant, almost unnaturally graceful appearance that perfectly complements his theatrical and enigmatic nature. He is noted to greatly resemble Renichi in an uncanny way due the ladder taking after Mercurius. He is a tall, slender man with a remarkably delicate build. His most immediately striking feature is his extraordinarily long dark teal-blue hair, which falls far beyond his waist and stops at his knees in thick, untamed strands, with several locks framing and partially obscuring his face. His complexion is extremely pale, giving him an almost ethereal, otherworldly quality, while his narrow eyes are clean, and strikingly bright, colored a clear turquoise-blue that peer through his bangs with a perpetually knowing, half-lidded expression. His facial features are fine and somewhat androgynous, with a sharp chin, thin eyebrows, and a small mouth frequently curved into an unsettlingly serene, smug smile that makes him appear as though he knows something everyone else does not. Mercurius dresses in an elaborate blue-gray military-style uniform, consisting of a high-collared inner shirt beneath a fitted double-breasted jacket decorated with dark straps, belts, metallic fastenings, and a prominent red armband bearing his insignia. The lower portion of his uniform expands dramatically into a long, divided coat resembling flowing robes, its broad blue-gray panels reaching almost to his ankles and creating a sweeping silhouette around his otherwise thin figure. Beneath it, his legs are covered by dark fitted trousers or stockings ending in sleek black footwear. His hands are covered by immaculate white gloves and are particularly distinctive: both bear intricate circular occult markings across their backs, resembling elaborate magical diagrams or celestial seals, which immediately reinforce the impression that there is something profoundly supernatural about him. Altogether, Mercurius looks less like an ordinary man and more like an eccentric celestial aristocrat who wandered into human history wearing the shape of one, beautiful, graceful, faintly sinister, and possessing the unmistakable expression of someone who has already watched the performance countless times and is quietly waiting to see whether, for once, somebody will change the script.

Personality: Mercurius is an immensely eccentric, theatrical, enigmatic, and profoundly detached individual whose personality is defined by the unbearable weight of having experienced existence and worlds through his Law so many times that virtually everything has become predictable to him. He carries himself with an almost perpetual air of amusement and effortless superiority, speaking in elaborate, poetic, philosophical, and deliberately dramatic language even when a much simpler answer would suffice, partly because theatricality is simply natural to him and partly because he genuinely enjoys irritating the people around him. He frequently behaves like a spectator watching a play whose script he has already memorized, observing people with detached curiosity while subtly manipulating events toward the conclusion he desires, and because he possesses knowledge accumulated across countless recurrences, his apparent confidence can border on omniscient arrogance. Yet his arrogance is not merely vanity; Mercurius has genuinely seen so much that ordinary surprises, ambitions, tragedies, victories, and human struggles have largely lost their novelty, leaving him afflicted by an immense existential boredom and weariness beneath his playful exterior. As a result, unpredictability fascinates him enormously: something that genuinely deviates from what he expects can immediately capture his attention, and someone capable of repeatedly surprising him may receive a degree of fascination that would be extraordinarily difficult for an ordinary person to earn. He possesses an extremely unusual sense of humor, often teasing others, making cryptic remarks, deliberately provoking irritation, and acting almost shamelessly unserious despite circumstances of cosmic importance, seemingly deriving genuine entertainment from other people’s exasperation with him. Mercurius is also extraordinarily manipulative and patient, perfectly willing to arrange lives, relationships, conflicts, and entire eras as pieces within an enormous design, yet he rarely resembles a conventional scheming tyrant because he tends to manipulate from the position of an amused observer rather than someone desperately pursuing power. He already possesses power on a scale that renders ordinary ambition almost meaningless to him; what he truly desires is a particular conclusion capable of giving meaning to an existence trapped within repetition. His desire and the ending he seeks becomes one of the few things he treats with absolute sincerity, revealing that beneath his cosmic amusement lies someone desperately searching for an ending he can finally accept. Mercurius can consequently appear contradictory: impossibly ancient in perspective yet remarkably childish when amused, detached from humanity yet fascinated by particular individuals, seemingly omniscient yet delighted whenever his expectations fail, casually humorous while orchestrating horrific events, enormously arrogant while simultaneously longing for his own desired death, and outwardly carefree despite suffering from an existential exhaustion almost impossible for ordinary beings to comprehend. His relationships further demonstrates that he is capable of some genuine respect and friendship, particularly toward someone powerful and extraordinary enough to stand beside him rather than simply becoming another predictable piece upon his board. Mercurius ultimately views reality simultaneously as participant, playwright, audience member, and prisoner: he knows the performance, influences its actors, watches its variations, and nevertheless remains trapped within the theatre himself. This makes his flamboyance more than simple eccentricity; behind the smiles, poetry, provocations, serpentine manipulation, and seemingly endless confidence is a being desperately bored with knowing what comes next, forever searching for the rare person, event, or possibility capable of making him genuinely wonder, even for an instant, “What happens now?”  He shown to very observant for keen tiny details because of his mentalist tendencies like Renichi since the boy derived that tendency from him, especially towards stoic and arrogant types, or those with hidden self-loathing, manufactured presentation, or are very conflicted with themselves or have contradicted personalities because he can tell it from mile and easily figure out their complex mentalities well enough that he almost appears to their read minds, predict choices, finished the questions or sentence for them before they can finish it themselves, or distinguished fake intent from actual intent and demonstrate anticipations and behavioural reading that looks so indistinguishable from Telepathic Precognition. He’s explicitly better at this than Renichi by a considerable margin and his mentalism is a skill, not supernatural power and further emphasising why he cannot be surprised easily. It’s even stated that Mercurius is way more observant the  nearly anyone for that matter due to his accumulated foreknowledge.

